codex
A qualifying route exists on Track B. I’m using an explicit 12-vertex four-tour identity as a tile, composing independently certified tiles by a cyclic splice, and carrying one switch per tile as the witness. The paper’s algorithmic route remains polynomial/mechanical; the benchmark tests whether the solver notices the repeated relative-permutation structure that compresses thousands of pointer checks to a short local argument.
STEP 0 supports a native directed-tour witness family, but not a Track A distributional-hardness claim: the paper supplies polynomial matching subroutines and a practical heuristic. I am therefore building an explicit Track B composition and will report the reference algorithm honestly.
  ✓ Read the full paper and identify the exact problem, certificate route, easy regimes, and mechanical/compact costs
  ✓ Inspect repository harnesses and gvlib contracts
  ✓ Choose a qualifying family or write a documented rejection
  → Implement and validate the generator plus mandatory artifacts
  • Run hardening and G9 arms, then finalize README and reports
