# Complementary directed tours from arXiv:1901.09651

| Profile | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
| Native domain | combinatorics |
| Object regime | finite discrete |
| Computational core | CSP/SAT-style binary matching choices |
| Certificate | exact symbolic binary selector |
| Intuition | invariant: chord intersections couple matching switches |
| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |

This module implements the paper's native complementary-tour problem.  A solver is
given two directed Hamiltonian tours and the alternating components of their union.
It must choose one of the two perfect matchings on every component so that both
complementary cycle covers are Hamiltonian and differ from the inputs.  The checker
reconstructs both covers and traverses their directed cycles exactly.  The selector
encoding is licensed by Section 3, directed Steps 1–2, where the paper identifies a
bipartite perfect matching with a directed cycle cover.  Source: [Kozlova and
Nikolaev, *Simulated annealing approach to verify vertex adjacencies in the traveling
salesperson polytope*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.09651).

## Why Track B

This is not a Track A claim.  Theorem 1 states worst-case NP-completeness for deciding
nonadjacency of TSP-polytope vertices, but the displayed complementary-tour condition
is only sufficient, and the theorem says nothing about this generated distribution.
Section 6 also says that pyramidal inputs have a linear-time test and reports rapid
heuristic success on many random undirected inputs, so neither regime is used as a
hardness assertion.

The reference algorithm is a best-improvement specialization of Algorithms 1–3: it
uses the paper's sum-of-component-counts energy, evaluates every one-component switch,
and rebuilds/traverses the two covers.  It is polynomial on this family,
`O(n m^2)` for `m` components, and succeeds as expected.  Across eight shipping
instances it took 203 improving iterations, 7,408,512 exact successor visits, and
0.772315 seconds on this host.  With the invariant, a solver instead treats the
components as chords in tour X and finds a crossing partner; the intended shipping
route is bounded by 192 exact integer additions/comparisons.  The executable compact
route verified 20/20 shipping instances and used at most 13 such operations on that
sample.  The benchmark tests whether that compression is discovered without tools.
For this construction the reference energy is exactly `2 + 2q`, where `q` is the
number of crossing pairs assigned unequal selector bits, which explains its guaranteed
descent and the exact solution count below.

## Worked demo

This is `make_instance(n=12, nesting=0, seed=11)` in full:

```text
Find a different complementary decomposition of two directed Hamiltonian tours.

Definitions.
The vertices are the integers 0 through 11.
A directed Hamiltonian tour is a cyclic ordering of every vertex exactly once;
its arcs go from each listed vertex to the next, and from the last back to the first.
The two input tours below have no common arcs. Their union is therefore a directed
multigraph with two incoming and two outgoing arcs at every vertex.

The union has alternating perfect-matching components. Each listed component
contains two source vertices. One selector bit controls the whole component:
bit 0 assigns both outgoing arcs used by tour X to cover Z and both X-complementary
arcs from tour Y to cover W; bit 1 swaps those assignments. Consequently every
binary selector produces two directed cycle covers Z and W whose disjoint arc
multisets equal X union Y. Your selector is valid exactly when Z and W are each
one Hamiltonian tour and the pair is different from the input pair X,Y.

There are m=6 components, indexed 0 through 5. Output exactly m bits in
component-index order, with no separators. The first bit must be 0; this chooses
one canonical representative because complementing every bit only exchanges Z
and W. The all-zero word is forbidden because it reproduces X,Y. Bits are 0 or 1.

For structural inspection, each component line also gives its two endpoint
positions in tour X (positions are 0-indexed). Two such components are crossing
chords when their four endpoint positions alternate around the cyclic order. This
annotation is derived exactly from X and Y and does not change the selector semantics.

Tour X (cyclic vertex order):
1 11 2 8 7 4 6 5 0 9 10 3

Tour Y (cyclic vertex order):
8 2 4 0 5 6 9 1 3 10 11 7

Alternating components, as `index: source source | X-positions position position`:
0: 3 9 | X-positions 9 11
1: 0 6 | X-positions 6 8
2: 2 7 | X-positions 2 4
3: 1 10 | X-positions 0 10
4: 4 5 | X-positions 5 7
5: 8 11 | X-positions 1 3

Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as the exact binary word.
Example of the required 6-bit syntax: <answer>011111</answer>
Output nothing else inside the tags.
```

The valid answer used here is `<answer>001001</answer>`.

Here components 2 and 5 are crossing chords.  `verify(inst, "001001")` returns
`(True, "ok")`; deleting a bit gives `verify(inst, "00100") == (False,
"too short: expected 6 bits, got 5")`.  A person can solve this demo on paper by
checking the six chord intervals.  `render()` includes the same definitions and the
precise tagged output contract.

