index 373c7c47f0fb38bcf28ce8af21b9dc588f00bf97..b271a460b358621f1ca51d914e7854fd91372102
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -5,146 +5,137 @@
 | Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
 | Native domain | combinatorics |
 | Object regime | finite discrete |
-| Computational core | CSP/SAT-style binary matching choices |
-| Certificate | exact symbolic binary selector |
-| Intuition | invariant: chord intersections couple matching switches |
-| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Computational core | permutation |
+| Certificate | integer tuple of switch-component indices |
+| Intended intuition | decomposition into repeated relative-permutation tiles |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
 
-This module implements the paper's native complementary-tour problem.  A solver is
-given two directed Hamiltonian tours and the alternating components of their union.
-It must choose one of the two perfect matchings on every component so that both
-complementary cycle covers are Hamiltonian and differ from the inputs.  The checker
-reconstructs both covers and traverses their directed cycles exactly.  The selector
-encoding is licensed by Section 3, directed Steps 1–2, where the paper identifies a
-bipartite perfect matching with a directed cycle cover.  Source: [Kozlova and
-Nikolaev, *Simulated annealing approach to verify vertex adjacencies in the traveling
-salesperson polytope*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.09651).
+## Problem and trust model
+
+The solver receives two explicit directed Hamiltonian tours and the nontrivial
+cycles of their relative successor permutation. Selecting a listed component
+swaps which input supplies the outgoing arc at all three of its tail vertices.
+The requested witness is a fixed-size set of components for which the selected
+arcs and their complement are both single Hamiltonian tours, different from the
+inputs. `verify` reconstructs the two successor permutations and traverses their
+cycles exactly; it never reads the planted answer.
 
+This is the native sufficient certificate in Lemma 1 of Kozlova and Nikolaev,
+[*Simulated annealing approach to verify vertex adjacencies in the traveling
+salesperson polytope*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.09651). The generator starts
+from an explicit 12-vertex four-tour identity. It composes copies by a cyclic
+splice, subdivides vertices, conjugates labels, swaps the inputs, and carries the
+known switch through every transformation. It does not search an emitted
+instance for its answer.
+
 ## Why Track B
 
-This is not a Track A claim.  Theorem 1 states worst-case NP-completeness for deciding
-nonadjacency of TSP-polytope vertices, but the displayed complementary-tour condition
-is only sufficient, and the theorem says nothing about this generated distribution.
-Section 6 also says that pyramidal inputs have a linear-time test and reports rapid
-heuristic success on many random undirected inputs, so neither regime is used as a
-hardness assertion.
+This is not a Track A distributional-hardness claim. The theorem quoted in
+Section 1 concerns worst-case NP-completeness of polytope nonadjacency, whereas
+the complementary-tour condition is only sufficient. Section 6 also identifies
+regimes that are easy in practice or in theory: pyramidal pairs have a linear-time
+test, and the paper's random undirected pairs were solved in essentially every
+experiment. Neither observation supports Track A for a planted distribution.
+
+The successful reference method here is an exact singleton relative-cycle scan:
+test every listed component by traversing both complete successor permutations.
+It costs `O(cV)` and, for shipping seed 20260905, used 24,128 pointer visits and
+0.0034 seconds on this host. Across the eight audit seeds it succeeds 8/8, as a
+Track B reference algorithm should. This complements the paper's mechanical
+annealing moves, whose directed cycle covers use Hopcroft–Karp matching in
+`O(sqrt(V) E)` per move (Sections 3–4).
 
-The reference algorithm is a best-improvement specialization of Algorithms 1–3: it
-uses the paper's sum-of-component-counts energy, evaluates every one-component switch,
-and rebuilds/traverses the two covers.  It is polynomial on this family,
-`O(n m^2)` for `m` components, and succeeds as expected.  Across eight shipping
-instances it took 203 improving iterations, 7,408,512 exact successor visits, and
-0.772315 seconds on this host.  With the invariant, a solver instead treats the
-components as chords in tour X and finds a crossing partner; the intended shipping
-route is bounded by 192 exact integer additions/comparisons.  The executable compact
-route verified 20/20 shipping instances and used at most 13 such operations on that
-sample.  The benchmark tests whether that compression is discovered without tools.
-For this construction the reference energy is exactly `2 + 2q`, where `q` is the
-number of crossing pairs assigned unequal selector bits, which explains its guaranteed
-descent and the exact solution count below.
+The compression is to recognize that the relative 3-cycles recur as independent
+12-vertex identities joined only by a cyclic splice. Local masks of weights
+0, 1, 3, and 4 preserve both tours; the planted route takes the certified
+weight-one mask in each tile. At the shipping preset this needs at most 116
+local exact checks, versus tens of thousands of full-tour pointer visits.
 
