Implemented a verified Track B generator for the paper’s complementary Hamiltonian-tour problem.

Files:

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/gen_1901_09651.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Results:

- All nine local gates pass.
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000.
- Exact shipping density: `351352 / 635013559600 ≈ 5.53e-7`.
- Five attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Track B reference scan: 8/8, `O(cV)`, 24,128 pointer visits on the measured shipping instance.
- G8: 60/60 symmetry checks, 20/20 unrelated instances distinct.
- Answer size: 51 characters, 13 elements; intended route: 116 operations.

The remaining blocker is external: all bare, hinted, and placebo oracle calls returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded` before any model answered. Consequently `submit.sh` correctly blocks submission because no valid multi-vendor hardened verdict exists. I did not write `REJECTED.md`; the family itself passed G, H on Track B, and V.
