Completed a verified Track B generator based on the paper’s complementary Hamiltonian-tour certificate ([arXiv:1901.09651](https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.09651)).

Deliverables:

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/gen_1901_09651.py:1)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/README.md:1)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Structural-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1901.09651/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Results:

- All G1–G9 gates pass.
- Shipping preset: `easy`, with 13 tiles and `C(52,13)` candidates.
- Exact valid-answer density: `351352 / 635013559600 ≈ 5.53×10⁻⁷`.
- Five failing attacks across eight seeds; successful Track B reference scan documented separately.
- Bare, structural-hint, and placebo oracle arms each scored `0/3`.
- Answer size: 51 characters, 13 elements; intended route: 116 exact checks.
- Canonical-key invariance and 20/20 unrelated-instance diversity verified.

The README records the main caveat: two bare oracle attempts exhausted their response budget without producing answers, and the family is deliberately easy for software, so this is strictly a no-tool compression benchmark—not a computational-hardness claim.
